analysis:
The only change in docs/reference/configuration.md is the date bump at head line 3 and a new 'Add-on hold' section at head lines 149-164. That section documents a local add-on hold IPC: DARKBLOOM_ADDON_SOCKET (test-only override of the socket path), DARKBLOOM_ADDON_DEV_IDENTITY (described as DEBUG-only, not read by release builds), a 30-600 s lease, a 120 s drain bound and a 60 s settle bound. Base units 4 and 5 (lines 477-736) are unchanged context covering cache, MiMo and memory settings; no change to the T-041 prefix-cache gates was observed.

No regression is identified from documentation alone. However, the add-on hold is a new local IPC surface across TB-003 (operator/local process vs provider). A local peer could hold the provider and pause serving, an availability concern related to T-016/T-057. A peer-identity policy (AddonPeerPolicy) is referenced but not supplied. The doc claims that release builds never read the dev identity and that neither variable is forwarded to the LaunchAgent. None of the following was supplied, so these claims are unverified:
- the AddonPeerPolicy.swift peer-identity enforcement and the DEBUG gating of the dev identity;
- the socket-path override and the 0700 parent creation in AddonHoldPaths.swift;
- the LaunchAgent.passthroughEnvironment exclusion;
- ProviderLoop+AddonHold.swift drain and lease behavior;
- addon-hold-ipc.md.

Integration should check:
- peer code-signing validation (team ID plus identifier) on the socket;
- that an operator-controlled socket path cannot be used for symlink or permission abuse;
- that the hold cannot pause or extend authorization or attestation proofs (TB-011/T-053);
- that drain under a hold preserves the fail-closed encryption semantics (T-008).

The threat model may need a TB-003 entry for the add-on hold IPC.
